<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Purandar Singha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1819">1819</a>, <a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1838">1838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Purandar Singha was the last king of the Ahom kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. Installed by a court faction in 1818, he was driven out a year later by Burmese forces who restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrakanta-singha">Chandrakanta Singha</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> made him tributary ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-assam">Upper Assam</a> in 1833, but it annexed his territory in 1838, citing misrule and arrears of tribute, ending Ahom rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/purandar-singha.jpg" alt="Purandar Singha" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Durgaram betha · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purandar_Singha,_the_last_king_of_Assam.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Purandar Singha was born in 1807 at Silmari in British-ruled Bengal, a great-great-grandson of the Ahom king Rajeswar Singha. His grandfather and his father Brajanath Gohain had conspired against reigning kings and lived there as exiled princes in poverty. In 1818 Ruchinath Burhagohain, who held King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrakanta-singha">Chandrakanta Singha</a> responsible for bringing in the Burmese, invited Brajanath to claim the throne, and their force entered the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorhat">Jorhat</a> on 17 February 1818. Brajanath had coins struck in his own name, but the mutilation of one of his ears barred him from kingship, since an Ahom king had to be without blemish. His son, then ten years old, was acclaimed instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Real authority lay with Brajanath and Ruchinath, who removed the officers suspected of loyalty to Chandrakanta. A Burmese army of 30,000 sent by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> defeated the Assamese on 17 February 1819; Purandar, his father and Ruchinath fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a> with the valuables of the royal treasury, worth 3.5 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. After a second defeat on 11 June 1819 they escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. The Governor-General Lord Hastings refused to help. They recruited soldiers in Goalpara, Bengal and Bhutan, armed with the aid of a British trader, Robert Bruce, and fought both Chandrakanta and the Burmese before withdrawing in 1822.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-yandabo">Treaty of Yandabo</a> of 24 February 1826 the British held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley. Sensing discontent, they considered in 1832 restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-assam">Upper Assam</a> as a tributary princedom and chose Purandar over Chandrakanta; some historians think they expected a long-exiled prince to be the more grateful and submissive. From April 1833 he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorhat">Jorhat</a>, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiya">Sadiya</a> and Matak, for a tribute of 50,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. Within three years he fell behind in his payments and asked for a reduction. An enquiry by Captain Francis Jenkins found corruption and oppression, and the king himself amassing a personal fortune.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom was placed under direct British control in September 1838. Purandar stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorhat">Jorhat</a> and refused the pension of 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> a month granted in October 1838, hoping that a petition to the Governor-General would restore his state; it was turned down. When officials and adventurers dug into the royal tombs, the maidams, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charaideo">Charaideo</a> in search of treasure, he first protested and then opened many tombs himself. He died on 1 October 1846 and was entombed at Jorhat, at the place now called Raja Maidam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Purandar Singha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Company briefly installed <b>Purandar Singha</b> as a tributary ruler of Upper Assam in 1833 but annexed his territory in 1838, ending Ahom rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
